  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nFlow aloft turns more SW for Wed, bringing moisture back into the\nregion from the south. A few showers and storms look to develop \nin the afternoon over Hudspeth and the Otero Mesa. Gusts to 40 mph\nand brief downpours are possible during the afternoon. Mostly dry\nconditions are forecast for El Paso and SW New Mexico with light \nS-SW winds.\n\nMoisture levels rise further for Thu as a shortwave trough pushes\nthrough NW Mexico. PWs climb towards 1\" during the day, focusing \nover SE areas for rain chances and flash flood concerns. Scattered\nshowers and isolated storms are forecast with repeated rounds of \nactivity possible into the overnight. Isolated rain amounts of \n1-2\" are likely through Thu night, favoring E of the US 54 \ncorridor. Most likely rainfall is 0.1-0.25\". Gusty winds and small\nhail are possible as instability and shear will be low compared \nto what we saw last week with our severe event. The flood threat \nlowers as you go west. This system exits Fri AM with a weaker \nshortwave arriving behind it Fri night. This will bring more \nscattered showers mainly to western areas Fri afternoon and night.\nThe flood threat shouldn't be as high late Fri with this \ndisturbance with generally light rain rates expected. \n\nHalloween dries out as a weak ridge translates over the region. \nMostly cloudy skies are forecast for Sat with seasonal temps and \nlight SW winds. Another Pacific trough moves into the Desert SW on\nSun, bringing record moisture to the region. PWs are modeled to \nreach near 1.2\", which is 3-4 std deviations above normal (0.45\").\nRain chances and the flood threat will favor western areas and \nthe high terrain through Sun night with lower chances for Texas. \nBreezy W winds also look to accompany the low Sun/Mon with cooler \ntemps behind the Pacific front. A quiet, dry stretch of weather is\nmodeled into the middle of next week as upper ridging builds from\nthe west.",
